tel 1.0

base virility
import soundness

info   Unix manual pages as a typed document tree, serialized to roff with correct escaping
detail
    _Virility_ builds a Unix manual page as a value — a `Roff` document of titled sections,
    paragraphs, tagged paragraphs, examples and indented blocks, with bold and italic runs —
    and serializes it to the roff source that `man` renders, using the man macro package
    (`.TH`, `.SH`, `.SS`, `.P`, `.TP`, `.EX`/`.EE`, `.RS`/`.RE`).

    Escaping is the library's reason to exist: text written into a roff document by hand is
    easily misread as markup. Every `-` in prose becomes `\-`, so that an option such as
    `--verbose` stays searchable and copyable in the rendered page; every `\` becomes
    `\[rs]`; a line beginning with `.` or `'`, which roff would take for a control line, is
    protected with `\&`; and a newline inside a line becomes a space.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val page = Roff
      ( t"greet", 1, t"2026-08-17", t"greet 1.0", t"User Commands",
        List
          ( Roff.Block.Section
              ( t"Name", List(Roff.Block.Paragraph(Roff.Inline.plain(t"greet - say hello"))) ),
            Roff.Block.Section
              ( t"Options",
                List
                  ( Roff.Block.Tagged
                      ( List(Roff.Inline.bold(t"--loud")),
                        Roff.Inline.plain(t"Greet in capital letters.") ) ) ) ) )

    page.serialize
    // .TH "GREET" "1" "2026-08-17" "greet 1.0" "User Commands"
    // .SH "Name"
    // greet \- say hello
    // .SH "Options"
    // .TP
    // \fB\-\-loud\fP
    // Greet in capital letters.
    ```

    Exoskeleton's manpage support generates its pages through this library, from the
    program's own command-line definitions. Virility has a single source set,
    `virility.core`, and everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella.

refer virility.Roff
refer virility.Roff.Block
refer virility.Roff.Inline

entry .Roff   a Unix manual page: its `.TH` title line and a list of blocks, serialized as roff
  detail
      A `Roff` is one manual page. Its fields become the arguments of the `.TH` line which
      begins the page: the `#title` (rendered in capitals), the `#section` number, and the
      optional `#date`, `#source` (the program and its version) and `#manual` (the title of
      the manual it belongs to, such as `User Commands`). Its `#blocks` are the body.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Roff(t"grep", 1).serialize                                  // .TH "GREP" "1"
      Roff(t"grep", 1, Unset, Unset, t"User Commands").serialize
      // .TH "GREP" "1" "" "" "User Commands"
      ```

      Trailing unset arguments are dropped from the `.TH` line, and unset ones before a set
      one are written as empty quoted arguments. A `Roff` shows, and encodes as `Text`, as its
      `#serialize`d source, so it can be written to a file with `write`.
  refer virility.Roff.Block
  refer virility.Roff.Inline
  entry .showable   shows a page as its roff source
  entry .encodable   encodes a page as its roff source text
  entry .escape   escapes text for a roff text line
      Every `-` becomes `\-`, `\` becomes `\[rs]`, and a newline becomes a space. It does not
      protect a leading `.` or `'`: the blocks do that for each line they emit, so text
      passed through `.escape` alone and written at the start of a line still needs `\&`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Roff.escape(t"list-files")   // t"list\\-files"
      ```
  entry .quote   escapes and double-quotes text as a macro argument
      For the arguments of `.TH`, `.SH` and `.SS`. Inside the quotes, `"` becomes `\[dq]` and
      `\` becomes `\[rs]`, but `-` is left alone: a hyphen in a title or a date is prose, and
      an escaped hyphen in the `.TH` date defeats `mandoc`'s date parsing.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Roff.quote(t"2026-08-17")   // t"\"2026-08-17\""
      ```
  entry .Inline   a run of text within a line: plain, bold or italic
    detail
        An enum of three cases. `Plain(text)` is escaped text; `Bold(inlines)` and
        `Italic(inlines)` wrap a list of further runs in the `\fB…\fP` and `\fI…\fP` font
        changes, so styles may nest. The companion's `plain`, `bold` and `italic` make the
        common single-run forms.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val runs = List(Roff.Inline.bold(t"ls"), Roff.Inline.Plain(t" "), Roff.Inline.italic(t"file"))
        runs.map(_.serialize).join   // t"\\fBls\\fP \\fIfile\\fP"
        ```
    entry .plain   a list of one plain run of text, the form a paragraph's prose takes
    entry .bold   one bold run of plain text
    entry .italic   one italic run of plain text
    entry #serialize   the run as roff text, escaped and with its font changes
  entry .Block   a block of a manual page: a section, a paragraph, a tagged paragraph, an example or an indent
    detail
        An enum of the block structures of the man macros:

        ```tabular
        column  Case
        column  Macros
        column  Block
        row  `Section(title, blocks)`  `.SH`  a section, such as `Name`, `Synopsis` or `Options`
        row  `Subsection(title, blocks)`  `.SS`  a subsection
        row  `Paragraph(prose)`  `.P`  a paragraph of inline runs; as the first block of a section or subsection, the redundant `.P` is omitted
        row  `Tagged(tag, body)`  `.TP`  the shape of an option's entry: the tag on one line and its description indented beneath; an empty body emits only the tag
        row  `Example(lines)`  `.EX`, `.EE`  literal lines, each escaped and protected
        row  `Indented(blocks)`  `.RS`, `.RE`  indented blocks
        ```

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Roff.Block.Example(List(t"grep -r pattern .", t".hidden")).serialize
        // List(t".EX", t"grep \\-r pattern .", t"\\&.hidden", t".EE")
        ```
    entry #serialize   the block as lines of roff source
        Each text line which begins with `.` or `'` is protected with `\&`, so prose can
        never be taken for a control line.
  entry #title   the page's name, usually the command's, rendered in capitals on the `.TH` line
  entry #section   the manual section number: 1 for user commands, 5 for file formats, 8 for administration
  entry #date   the date of the page's last change, as text
  entry #source   the source of the program, usually its name and version
  entry #manual   the title of the manual the page belongs to, such as `User Commands`
  entry #blocks   the body of the page
  entry #serialize   the whole page as roff source, ending with a newline
